The anisotropic Wilson–Dirac tight-binding Hamiltonian on the cubic lattice is $H_{\mathrm{bulk}}=\epsilon( oldsymbol{k})\mathbf{1}+	au_{x}m( oldsymbol{k})+	au_{y}\sigma_{\mu}A_{\mu}\sin k_{\mu}$ with $m( oldsymbol{k})=m_{0}+2m_{2\mu}(1-\cos k_{\mu})$, where $\epsilon( oldsymbol{k})$ is an even function of momentum, $	au_{x},	au_{y}$ are orbital-spin Pauli matrices, $\sigma_{\mu}$ are real-spin Pauli matrices, $A_{\mu}$ are velocity parameters, $m_0$ is a mass parameter, and $m_{2\mu}$ are Wilson mass parameters. Uniaxial anisotropy is implemented by setting $m_{2\perp}
eq m_{2\parallel}$ and $A_{\perp}
eq A_{\parallel}$, where $\perp$ refers to the $z$-direction and $\parallel$ to the $x,y$-directions. For the phase diagram analysis, $\epsilon( oldsymbol{k})$ is set to zero. Under this condition, the model exhibits a phase diagram in the $(m_0/m_{2\parallel},\,m_{2\perp}/m_{2\parallel})$ plane that contains multiple strong topological insulator (STI, strong index $
u_0=1$) and weak topological insulator (WTI, $
u_0=0$) phases, characterized by $
u_0$, the weak indices $\vec{
u}=(
u_1,
u_2,
u_3)$, and an integer winding number $N_3$. The phase boundaries correspond to bulk gap closings at time-reversal-invariant momenta (TRIM: eight momenta $ oldsymbol{k}$ satisfying $- oldsymbol{k}= oldsymbol{k}+ oldsymbol{G}$ with reciprocal lattice vector $ oldsymbol{G}$). These boundaries allow engineering of $\vec{
u}$ by tuning $m_{2\perp}/m_{2\parallel}$; for example, along $m_0/m_{2\parallel}=-1$, the system transitions from STI $(
u_0=1,\vec{
u}=(0,0,0))$ to the WTI-A phase $(
u_0=0,\vec{
u}=(0,0,1))$ when $m_{2\perp}/m_{2\parallel}$ crosses $1/4$. This phase diagram and $N_3$ provide explicit parameter windows to realize desired combinations of strong and weak indices on the lattice.
